dices list for each state only temporarily, and when we compare two candidate solutions (include vs exclude), we can compare their full indices lists. Since we generate a new tuple by appending to the previous tuple, we can compute the comparison by comparing the two tuples directly. For each state, we only need to keep the best one, so we keep one tuple per state. That's acceptable for moderate sizes.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, I'll implement a hybrid: if n <= 20, brute force; else, use DP with memoization and full tuple storage. The public fixtures have n up to 6, so likely fine.\n\nI'll write the function accordingly.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate input: case is dict with keys 'lengths', 'scores', 'budget'. Ignore extra keys.\n2. n = len(lengths).\n3. If n <= 20: brute force.\n   - best_score = -inf, best_tokens = inf, best_indices = None.\n   - For mask in range(1<<n):\n        total_len = 0, total_score = 0, indices = []\n        for i in range(n):\n            if mask & (1<<i):\n                total_len += lengths[i]\n                total_score += scores[i]\n                indices.append(i)\n        if total_len <= budget:\n            # compare with best using (score, -tokens, indices) lexicographically? Actually compare score descending, then tokens ascending, then indices lexicographically ascending.\n            if (total_score > best_score) or (total_score == best_score and total_len < best_tokens) or (total_score == best_score and total_len == best_tokens and indices < best_indices):\n                best_score, best_tokens, best_indices = total_score, total_len, indices\n   - After loop, best_indices is list. Return {\"selected_indices\": best_indices, \"tokens_used\": best_tokens, \"total_score\": best_score}\n   - Note: if no subset fits? But there is always empty set? The empty set has tokens_used=0, score=0. So best will be at least that.\n4. Else (n > 20): DP with dictionary.\n   - Initialize dp as a dict: dp[0] = (0, 0, ())? Actually we'll use dp as list of dicts: dp[i] for after processing i items. But we can use a single dict for current states. Starting with dp = {0: (0, 0, ())} meaning tokens used=0, score=0, indices=().\n   - For each i from 0 to n-1:\n        new_dp = dp.copy()  # excluding current item\n        for tokens, (score, _, indices) in dp.items():\n            new_tokens = tokens + lengths[i]\n            if new_tokens <= budget:\n                new_score = score + scores[i]\n                new_indices = indices + (i,)\n                # compare with existing entry in new_dp for new_tokens\n                existing = new_dp.get(new_tokens)\n                if existing is None:\n                    new_dp[new_tokens] = (new_score, new_tokens, new_indices)\n                else:\n                    old_score, old_tokens, old_indices = existing\n                    # compare: higher score wins, then lower tokens, then lexicographic indices\n                    if (new_score > old_score) or (new_score == old_score and new_tokens < old_tokens) or (new_score == old_score and new_tokens == old_tokens and new_indices < old_indices):\n                        new_dp[new_tokens] = (new_score, new_tokens, new_indices)\n        dp = new_dp\n   - After processing all items, we need to find best among all states (any tokens <= budget). Since we stored only states with exact tokens, the empty set is in dp with tokens=0. So iterate over all values in dp, apply same comparison.\n   - Best selection: maybe also consider states with less tokens? But we have all exact tokens states, so we have all possibilities. So best is the best value across dp.values().\n   - Then return dict.\n\nNote: In DP we stored tokens_used as part of the tuple, but we can just store score and indices, tokens are the key. But for comparison between same token budget, we don't need to compare tokens because they are same. But for final comparison across different tokens, we need to compare overall best. So we can keep the tuple as (score, tokens, indices). But we already have tokens as key. However for final best, we need to compare across tokens. So we can store (score, indices) in dp, and for final best, we also need tokens. We can store as tuple (score, indices) and get tokens from key. But for tie-breaking, we need tokens. So better to store (score, tokens, indices). That's redundant but fine.\n\nIn DP update, when comparing two states for same token count, they have same tokens so just compare score then indices. But for final best across different tokens, we need to compare score, then tokens, then indices. So we can have a compare function.\n\nImplementation details: Use nested functions.\n\nEdge cases: budget may be 0 or less? lengths positive, so only empty set. scores list same length as lengths.\n\nMake sure to return JSON serializable types.\n\nNow, produce the final response with implementation as JSON string.\n\nAlso note: The case payload may contain '_case_nonce' or other keys, ignore.\n\nNow write the Python source code as a string.\n\nI'll write the code with docstring and comments.\n\nLet's implement."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1134,"completion_tokens":4833,"total_tokens":5967,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4085},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":110},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}